 sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-2)=6$ and $f(3)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [2,6].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$2$ and $6$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/567a6a601c80d1af5adcb10188d6ae4de83aa154505d59945cadbc226f05eb4b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48334/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48334"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection/5931a9eac9bbc8d732a749add6d47153f548d4592a71fb47fe521d1edc3e9a00" :answer/id #uuid "6e8cd68e-a8ef-5127-95ed-9b0842bcb7f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c\\in (-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection/b47381d1a40747fa8670e8262ef36ff0bb189ea4ea638b5bd30098398b7b62e8" :answer/id #uuid "9dc6a1ed-4a6e-58e5-96da-4135e6742d44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c\\in (1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection/692c02b46caf7d373aa05b5c296fe34d3c126d66545ca3ebdc228ff6922361b6" :answer/id #uuid "94ff9198-9947-5944-97a9-0dd9dfcf8170" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c\\in [-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection/bbd2446baa14c2811d5eb918cc8f4410ceba0ab03ded6ebcbe0be20fea538622" :answer/id #uuid "eb8f2b32-52bb-5576-8aad-782e67498f4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c\\in [-2,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection/86e88bcf7892e0c1ba1643cdeaee59c45df8f266ccdaf6d2c2c769a4c78cbfb3" :answer/id #uuid "6470bc4a-3c6f-5070-a2b5-42c2f3a02b76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c\\in [1,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "87093c70-1501-5edf-80d2-2485e63c4a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48361/selection/bbd2446baa14c2811d5eb918cc8f4410ceba0ab03ded6ebcbe0be20fea538622" :answer-field/choices ["q-48361/selection/5931a9eac9bbc8d732a749add6d47153f548d4592a71fb47fe521d1edc3e9a00" "q-48361/selection/b47381d1a40747fa8670e8262ef36ff0bb189ea4ea638b5bd30098398b7b62e8" "q-48361/selection/692c02b46caf7d373aa05b5c296fe34d3c126d66545ca3ebdc228ff6922361b6" "q-48361/selection/bbd2446baa14c2811d5eb918cc8f4410ceba0ab03ded6ebcbe0be20fea538622" "q-48361/selection/86e88bcf7892e0c1ba1643cdeaee59c45df8f266ccdaf6d2c2c769a4c78cbfb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48361" :question/id #uuid "23995f2c-e036-515a-946d-e63146f2696f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48361" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4906a1de7cc1eb78bbff845fe6b03b024b47c46e84c801609e8da40a5d37c864.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $2$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(2)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(2)=2.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,2]$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/455863da8f126505f018a356f5f89bbbea81f1e1e90e803a51390bed22514a9a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48361/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48361"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47058"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff" :answer/id #uuid "f2735f17-a908-5f7d-8d25-e224647147f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection/53cb6fffc80c9612581a177458e2cc038c228df19ee12acca12be555f8ff8b68" :answer/id #uuid "807fe3df-ebb5-5636-8b13-6f8024967c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection/5ecba1d45a9d16913fd88aa066593c45c993e24db75e55ae5baf151dd6db7997" :answer/id #uuid "746c1e84-e34f-5197-9128-967f77117a2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection/4e2429b00a3baaac76f6e33158fbcdff5dcf0af49a1b90a9dac6995df4919225" :answer/id #uuid "c49851eb-00b0-58fb-8cc7-ad896c139ece" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection/06cab54b54cd66b0f5fe1d93b2cddb64dabdf1b13c526982d02fcdb490d64afa" :answer/id #uuid "f13b08ba-d624-5183-9813-ae2879eb1291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d475312d-3f30-5553-bd62-b0f81030b07d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105684/selection/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-105684/selection/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff" "q-105684/selection/53cb6fffc80c9612581a177458e2cc038c228df19ee12acca12be555f8ff8b68" "q-105684/selection/5ecba1d45a9d16913fd88aa066593c45c993e24db75e55ae5baf151dd6db7997" "q-105684/selection/4e2429b00a3baaac76f6e33158fbcdff5dcf0af49a1b90a9dac6995df4919225" "q-105684/selection/06cab54b54cd66b0f5fe1d93b2cddb64dabdf1b13c526982d02fcdb490d64afa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105684" :question/id #uuid "84e2e7d1-ebc5-58ab-8dc8-1f209b929dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-105684" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(4)=66,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,66].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[2,66].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-105684/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105684"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78510/selection/5ecba1d45a9d16913fd88aa