mediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-3,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-3)=-7$ and $f(1)=1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-7,1].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=-2$ belongs to the interval $[-7,1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/3a9cf79e319e3a4a8d7d5195d65bc01e69bceb3721c29ca905132c1a607ba413.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105666/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105666"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "46a00ddd-ce6d-521f-92c2-7d27cd7c051c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "597bab38-2d9b-588c-a7f5-61c88180e414" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "dcac1b18-cba9-569a-bc93-42a1315b185e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e8e4ca36-287a-559d-8dad-2573df10897d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2178564b-e988-5ca6-bac5-e94928b8d1bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "661a7cd6-21e2-5ad0-ae21-0d83b89886d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47056/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-47056/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-47056/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-47056/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-47056/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-47056/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47056" :question/id #uuid "8186883d-213b-5f00-9b7b-9a8de4d9c22e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47056" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47056/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8f4441f5-dc2d-5e71-ab3e-a57b7a0fe01d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "48cb2c3b-dffd-557f-bd70-629eb3b2c000" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "0c379d93-f7e2-5e39-837e-57b2d4f9010b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4e9ee9ef-0754-5612-bf7b-3fc3ee107458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6cf50740-3fea-51db-bc11-dbc18ddd17cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "173e3de0-c9dd-5b6a-aeb4-f1f28a1cd4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-78542/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-78542/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-78542/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-78542/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-78542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78542" :question/id #uuid "c2993211-5bf5-5455-954d-b0eb6a795598" :question/math-academy-id "q-78542" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e23cfe1d-ebd5-592f-b1a0-1a58e6bda67a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d451ce96-5287-58be-b94e-8e041ca0b047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2cff2729-d494-5f57-8918-7b167f12f627" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "dd262be0-5e1e-524e-9218-f4bcc266ba64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c786ebaa-6864-5bdf-a4b3-f0bbf9c7aeb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f99c1c86-001c-57f6-b815-e2f99a7fd7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78537/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-78537/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-78537/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-78537/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-78537/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-78537/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78537" :question/id #uuid "c832a0ab-c8ed-58dd-a542-b7df2a173528" :question/math-academy-id "q-78537" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $f(2)=3$ and $f(3)=1.$ Since $2\\in [1,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,6],$ we have $f(3)=1$ and $f(6)=4.$ Since $2\\in [1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $f(6)=4$ and $f(7)=0.$ Since $2\\in [0,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=2$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,6]$ and $[6,7].$  Therefore, $f(x)=2$ has at least three solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78537/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78537"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "589418da-2615-552b-821c-9ed20807450e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "36d2032f-b374-5d08-9044-af2a46fc1cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d044327e-10ef-5f42-a791-2c1c006fb57e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "fb430401-9119-5b8c-896c-d1e45a5dff02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "b67ecf48-b76f-5d47-8c4d-011d0b118177" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e63cee1-8f88-5bfc-a6ed-546bee2045b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105701/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-105701/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-105701/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-105701/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-105701/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-105701/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105701" :question/id #uuid "07c30a16-0504-5c16-ba6b-3435983938c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105701" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $g(2)=-2$ and $g(3)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-2,1],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=1$ and $g(4)=-3.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $g(4)=-3$ and $g(5)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,6],$ we have $g(5)=1$ and $g(6)=0.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $g(6)=0$ and $g(7)=2.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-1$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,4]$  and $[4,5].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-1$ has at least three solutions on $[2,7].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105701/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105701"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344761/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "248186dc-69bc-588d-af4f-ea72c61b7aad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344761/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3de3cae7-60c1-589f-b727-08a1c3ebacba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344761/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344761/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344761" :question/id #uuid "efc73626-18fa-5f9c-868c-bc8028827690" :question/math-academy-id "q-344761" :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $p(x)=2?$\n\nThe equation $p(x)=2$ has at least {{field-1}} solutions." :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344761/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d4d80a1c-a681-5a79-9f63-0925c626bd3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e6ae97dc-9ad5-5174-b76e-8850f13d23f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "63a4ae8c-4442-5de5-94b6-232ef617a28f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "8610a7aa-bf37-5c1d-bb95-fbcd49ab1177" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b4ad231f-aed0-54ef-ae98-6e126fd6c504" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "44ed66c8-4595-521b-a539-85b6b3486dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47016/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-47016/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-47016/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-47016/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-47016/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-47016/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47016" :question/id #uuid "607354a6-04e1-596c-a6fa-ef51f5a0c65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47016" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47016/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47016"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "cd90d2dc-2192-55f8-9f49-2009b262e204" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "6f411143-f108-53f2-85c4-09d79aa535f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "64ac0818-2e3b-543b-a8cd-d46bd1c275c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ad8eb979-e97f-5324-9249-e882c3f93a3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1adffa92-2a25-54f0-a38d-b3db45a7d65c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b333f02-6466-58f8-9986-a863aba1c44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47055/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-47055/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-47055/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-47055/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-47055/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-47055/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47055" :question/id #uuid "697ba05b-3399-5081-bfb5-548a407d6a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-47055" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[-1,4]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47055/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47055"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" :answer/id #uuid "265faa65-e740-5f6a-adaf-a25daec9852f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" :answer/id #uuid "344c5f03-f255-5fdd-9978-57b07397eecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection/efaf615f81b16e3ebf719aebc65dc121df2380a0a056e43e76b33c5dbef23845" :answer/id #uuid "8eaa55f4-8be3-5d67-b223-84127e46ec87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection/f92af53e1021b91c6026b26979abf2a4d8b656043c59928b749366d16f0d4b08" :answer/id #uuid "06bc2954-d765-5a6a-919f-8b4d0f08e6d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection/8406158c0997d20670e2a0b495f23aaf50f27fbfe1747535b5e0d43156109f26" :answer/id #uuid "7da63e35-551e-597e-8680-25e779f087cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6057ac87-2a36-51ab-a6bc-c8d90498e45f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47077/selection/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" :answer-field/choices ["q-47077/selection/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" "q-47077/selection/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" "q-47077/selection/efaf615f81b16e3ebf719aebc65dc121df2380a0a056e43e76b33c5dbef23845" "q-47077/selection/f92af53e1021b91c6026b26979abf2a4d8b656043c59928b749366d16f0d4b08" "q-47077/selection/8406158c0997d20670e2a0b495f23aaf50f27fbfe1747535b5e0d43156109f26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077" :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47077/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6