0588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47035" :question/id #uuid "2fb92b7b-dc10-5228-9860-a0045fd149f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47035" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n\n- $[-5,-3]$\n- $[-3,-2]$\n- $[-2,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47035/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47035"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection/e7088ee40ca765ea6029cd5183e3fac1a4fae4297c66bb51617f7333bcbfb353" :answer/id #uuid "dbe959cd-e4ab-592a-8976-7e1d2bdcb7dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-8,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection/1e3202d6e4762a1820856ea4b44ebeaf07b8555e13da1c6600d05afc59d306d7" :answer/id #uuid "cd0e4cbd-0cf5-5247-bb9f-42420165d644" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection/78ce315eede7ac6d6f6ded12ad514448276d3392923ee7da4b43ea7dd1d73140" :answer/id #uuid "5a65cea0-8ee6-5597-b009-0b07d92e7328" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-4,-2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection/8ed89aefef5733152fd7c3036e00668a21237a8aa673e9b855dc474695ae9dea" :answer/id #uuid "94c8694a-e26a-5ff3-8392-10ee69cb0113" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-5,-3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection/4943435f9213e675a5d6e1800172996784d111c58afd270cf3789a255a4aaea9" :answer/id #uuid "502822aa-bdd4-5e02-8621-c427876da4bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f316c664-e162-5146-9216-285295eb0098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105710/selection/4943435f9213e675a5d6e1800172996784d111c58afd270cf3789a255a4aaea9" :answer-field/choices ["q-105710/selection/e7088ee40ca765ea6029cd5183e3fac1a4fae4297c66bb51617f7333bcbfb353" "q-105710/selection/1e3202d6e4762a1820856ea4b44ebeaf07b8555e13da1c6600d05afc59d306d7" "q-105710/selection/78ce315eede7ac6d6f6ded12ad514448276d3392923ee7da4b43ea7dd1d73140" "q-105710/selection/8ed89aefef5733152fd7c3036e00668a21237a8aa673e9b855dc474695ae9dea" "q-105710/selection/4943435f9213e675a5d6e1800172996784d111c58afd270cf3789a255a4aaea9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105710" :question/id #uuid "bc3e6bf9-50aa-5413-821b-b19c043d5f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105710" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+5x+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,3]$ is allowed. The intervals $[-8,-3)$ and $(-2,1]$ are not closed, while $[-5,-3]$ and $[-4,-2]$ contain the point $x=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-105710/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6753"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-114"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-115"] :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-116"] :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
